WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.150
Chances are pretty good that you've already
seen Avengers: Endgame and don't need this

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.890
spoiler warning — but, as you no doubt know,
Tony Stark doesn't make it out of Endgame

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.380
alive.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.280
Since so much of the Marvel Cinematic Universe
has been built around Stark and his armor-clad

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.280
alter ego, his absence leaves a pretty big
vacuum.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.820
But will it stay that way forever?

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.320
Maybe...maybe not.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.320
Here's how Iron Man just might return to the
MCU, in one form or another.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.580
One of the best things about having a hero
whose powers come from a suit of armor is

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.429
that almost anyone can wear it, assuming they're
the kind of person who doesn't mind flying

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:34.600
around with rocket-boots and shooting bad
guys with repulsor rays.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.140
If that's the case, then there's no better
candidate for the new Iron Man than Colonel

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.390
Jim Rhodes, who already has plenty of experience
in the suit.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.480
"I'm gonna have to hang onto your suit for
a minute, OK?"

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.100
"Not OK.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.360
Not OK with that."

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.680
"Wasn't a question."

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.040
That's not what makes Rhodey the obvious choice,
though.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.700
In comics, he's been better known as War Machine
for decades, but Rhodey's first major story

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.920
came when he took over the identity of Iron
Man while Tony was struggling with alcoholism.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.399
His tenure as the Armored Avenger lasted for
four years, and even saw him as the version

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.240
of Iron Man who went off to fight in the Secret
Wars event.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.880
Rhodey has the added bonus of being a known
quantity among MCU fans.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.940
Don Cheadle might've replaced Terrence Howard
with the second movie in the Iron Man franchise,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.100
but the character has been around since the
first Iron Man film.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.670
Then again, that might be a detriment.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.259
Cheadle's the same age as Downey, and while
that obviously didn't keep him from some epic

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.760
superhero action in Endgame, filmmakers might
be looking for someone younger going forward.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.700
Endgame paid off plenty of plot points and
lived up to its promise of being the end of

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.240
an era, but the MCU shows no sign of slowing
down anytime soon.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.220
So it makes sense that Endgame would also
lay down a little foreshadowing of its own.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.960
If that's the case, then there might be a
good reason why Morgan Stark, Tony's five

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.260
year-old daughter, is introduced to us wearing
one of Tony's helmets and a toy Repulsor,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:54.079
and why a huge chunk of the movie is devoted
to Tony reconciling with his own father.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.240
From helping found S.H.I.E.L.D. to helping
found the Avengers and Pepper showing up as

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.090
Rescue, superheroism is a family business
for the Starks.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.360
There's no reason why Morgan couldn't eventually
carry on that legacy.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.360
The obvious problem there is that Morgan Stark
is five years old.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.980
It's also completely uncharted territory.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.100
Unlike Rhodey, who's a well-known quantity
as Iron Man, Tony Stark has never had a kid

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:14.800
in the comics.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.660
There is an existing Morgan Stark on the page,
but he's Tony's nefarious cousin who constantly

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.240
attempts to take over his business.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.020
If the MCU wants to get really weird, there's
one very unlikely character who could take

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.660
up the armor as the all-new Iron Man: Peter
Parker.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.180
Spider-Man is, after all, one of Marvel's
most popular characters.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.240
And with Into the Spider-Verse's critical
acclaim, he's one of the only superheroes

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.770
who can carry two simultaneous film franchises
that, for now at least, have nothing to do

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:41.540
with each other.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.880
In fact, the latest trailer for Spider-Man:
Far From Home heavily implies that Peter is

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.580
actually thinking along these lines:

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.510
"The world needs the next Iron Man!"

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.480
So yeah, it could definitely happen — but
it's almost certain that it won't.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.360
Blending his role with Iron Man's wouldn't
really do either one of those brands any favors,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.880
and would probably dilute the elements that
make each of them work on their own.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.300
Still, there's a level where it would actually
make sense.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.280
From the moment he stepped onto the screen
in Captain America: Civil War, Tony has mentored

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.650
Peter, looking to avoid making the same mistakes
his father made when it came to dealing with

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.080
a teenage super-genius.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:13.840
"All right.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:14.840
I've run out of patience.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.360
Underoos!"

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.960
There's an almost familial connection there,
which we see in Endgame when the only people

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.140
who really get to mourn for Tony on the battlefield
are Peter Parker and Pepper Potts.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.290
Tony has even provided Spider-Man with his
equipment, giving him the high-tech version

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.650
of his familiar suit that he wears in Homecoming,
and the "Iron Spider" armor that we see him

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.400
wearing in Infinity War and Endgame.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:43.760
It's a relationship that they've never really
had in the comics, and if he was any other

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.080
character, Peter would be the obvious choice
to take over.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.560
As it stands, we're definitely getting a further
examination of that mentoring relationship,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.320
but it's far more likely to end with Spider-Man
deciding to strike out on his own.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.931
If the MCU really wants to move forward and
put a younger character into the armor, they

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.960
don't need to go for something as wild as
Peter Parker: Iron Man.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.620
The comics have served up a ready-made replacement
in the form of Riri Williams, better known

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:07.380
as Ironheart.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:13.460
Introduced in 2016, Riri is a teenage genius
from Chicago who reverse-engineered Stark

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.200
technology and built her own suit of Iron
Man armor.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.600
Since Tony Stark was dead at the time and,
as is often the case in comics, he got better

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.180
she wound up fighting crime, assisted by an
AI version of Tony.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.260
Eventually, after a meeting with Pepper, she
wound up creating her own suit from scratch

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.320
and taking the identity of Ironheart.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.420
As far as replacements go, she'd definitely
be an interesting one.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:37.340
But the fact that she has an established separate
superheroic identity that's already appeared

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.889
in cartoons would seem to make that unlikely.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:43.480
Still, there's one really cool connection
to the roots of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.130
Back in 2008, Tony Stark built his first suit
of Iron Man armor in a cave.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.600
Riri built hers in the garage.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.780
Echoing those roots in a different form would
be a nice way to go forward.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.260
Maybe Marvel doesn't actually want to replace
Tony Stark as Iron Man.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.340
Maybe they want to bring him back, but also
make sure that they don't undermine the sacrifice

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.560
he made to defeat Thanos.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.500
If so, there's one way that they can have
their armor-plated cake and eat it, too: they

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.880
could give us the film version of Teen Tony.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.200
The comics killed off Tony Stark in 1996 after
revealing he was secretly a villain and attempted

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.520
to attract new readers by replacing him with
a teenaged version of Tony from another dimension.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.470
Like the original Tony, he had to wear the
armor to keep his heart beating, but did so

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.140
as a cool '90s youth who didn't have a mustache.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.340
He also didn't have much success in the whole
"get people to read this comic" thing, and

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:32.700
was killed off via heroic sacrifice ten months
later, quickly becoming one of those weird

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.720
comics things that we just don't talk about
much.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.810
Still, it's been 22 years, and that's enough
time to give this idea another shot by casting

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.420
a young CW star as the all-new Tony Stark.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:46.840
We even have more than a few hints that the
MCU might be introducing a full-on multiverse

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:51.550
as of Spider-Man: Far from Home, giving him
or any other alternate Tony the perfect opportunity

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.000
to jump into the MCU.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.400
"You're saying there's a multiverse?"

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.000
Is teen Tony going to happen?

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.760
Probably not, but, that's exactly what we
would've said if you told us ten years ago

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:04.430
that Rocket Raccoon, Crossbones, and M'Baku
were going to be in the same

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.560
movie, and that it was going to be one of
the biggest blockbusters of all time.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.740
Time makes fools of us all.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.280
There's another alternate version of Iron
Man who seems like he's begging for a comeback:

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.680
Arno Stark, the Iron Man of 2020.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:21.420
He was introduced in 1984, when 2020 still
seemed like the far-off future.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:26.030
The basic idea is that he was a younger cousin
of Tony Stark's who used a scary-looking version

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.300
of the Iron Man suit to act as a mercenary.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.800
Thanks to the magic of time travel, Iron Man
2020 wound up fighting Spider-Man in 1986

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.600
and Iron Man in 1990 in what was a surprisingly
uneventful career as a supervillain.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:43.120
With 2020 on the horizon, it might not surprise
you to learn that Marvel has been laying the

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.680
seeds for Arno to make a return in the comic
books.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.540
Most recently, it's turned out that Tony was
actually adopted, and that Howard and Maria

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:55.090
Stark's first child, Arno, had been the subject
of genetic experimentation by an alien robot,

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:56.400
which caused them to adopt Tony.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.380
It's… complicated.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:03.560
Arno eventually made his return as a relatively
heroic character who teamed up with Tony,

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.300
and is currently floating around the Marvel
Universe, presumably waiting to live up to

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:07.800
his name.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.920
Obviously, that's some complicated stuff even
by Marvel standards.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.980
But hey: the opportunity to use Iron Man 2020
in 2020 only comes around once.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.860
If Tony Stark himself is going to make a comeback,
then there's an easy way to pull it off without

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:25.400
cheapening his death in Endgame: Just have
Tony return as an artificial intelligence.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.090
This has been done before in the comics, most
recently with the aforementioned A.I.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:33.040
Tony that helped Riri Williams build her armor
and served as her on-board computer.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.920
Of course, in the comics, living artificial
intelligence characters are a dime a dozen,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.060
but the MCU isn't far off itself.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.460
Friday and Karen, the onboard A.I. for Spider-Man's
Stark-designed suit, both have distinct personalities,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:48.639
and J.A.R.V.I.S., the Iron Man suit's original
A.I., was even able to evolve into a full-on

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.320
superhero in Age of Ultron, when it became
the Vision.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.090
This all depends on how truly done with the
role Robert Downey Jr. is.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.620
But all things considered, it seems like a
pretty likely way to get Tony back into the

00:07:58.620 --> 00:07:59.120
mix.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.620
A holographic Tony serving as the onboard
A.I.for a character like Riri or Morgan or

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.700
Rhodey would be a nice way to bridge the gap
to a new Iron Man.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.340
Or he could even control an empty suit of
armor himself.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:13.580
If Iron Man is going to come back, then there's
nothing that says he has to be a good guy.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.479
A new Iron Man could use Tony's name and technology
for sinister ends.

00:08:17.479 --> 00:08:21.009
That's actually the premise of one of the
most well-known and beloved Iron Man stories,

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:21.760
"Armor Wars."

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:25.600
The premise is that Tony finds out that his
tech has been used by armored villains like

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.419
the Titanium Man, the Crimson Dynamo, and
even Stilt-Man, and he goes all-out to destroy

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:33.260
their armor so that his creations can't be
used for evil.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.919
The MCU obviously lacks the sheer number of
armored enemies that the comics have built

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:41.980
up over the past 60 years, but between Obadiah
Stane's recreation of the armor in Iron Man,

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:46.460
Justin Hammer's mass-produced knockoffs in
Iron Man 2, and all of those pieces of the

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:50.360
armor that were destroyed in Iron Man 3, there's
enough of it out there that it's not implausible

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.490
that someone could build their own, especially
if they had an uninterrupted five-year span

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:55.280
to work on it.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.860
The idea of Pepper and Rhodey teaming up to
save Tony's legacy is compelling, and a villain

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.500
co-opting Tony's signature line...

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:03.420
"I am Iron Man."

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.620
...is almost so easy that it's tough to imagine
not doing it.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.500
Plus, dealing with the idea of the legacy
these heroes leave, blurring the definitive

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:16.600
lines between heroes and villains that we've
seen in Endgame, and examining the Iron Man

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:20.390
suit as a weapon whose morality is determined
entirely by its user...all that would be an

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.380
interesting way to move forward in a world
without Tony Stark.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.200
One of Marvel's most popular characters in
recent years has been Squirrel Girl.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:27.920
No, really.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.940
She's the star of a long-running and award-winning
comic, and she's set to appear as the lead

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:34.080
in an upcoming New Warriors TV series.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:37.700
While Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D. and the Netflix
Marvel shows are technically set in the Marvel

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.430
Cinematic Universe, TV and movie projects
have always been pretty separate.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:46.420
If, however, the MCU wants to tie things together,
there's one way they could generate some intense

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.680
brand synergy and recreate an iconic moment
from the comics.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.240
Just have the new Iron Man be based on the
scene where an evil duplicate of Squirrel

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.880
Girl kidnaps Tony Stark and convinces her
rodent pals to crawl into his armor and scurry

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:01.600
around trying to operate it, with predictably
disastrous results.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.380
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.580
Plus, even more Looper videos about the MCU
are coming soon.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.760
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

